"""Item-specific grammatical explanations, alternatives and diagnoses."""
N={
'44.1':'''**Gāð** and **bycgað** are plural commands to the sons. **Ūs** is dative beneficiary, “for us”; **sumne dǣl mętes** is the accusative amount bought, with genitive mętes specifying the food. The amount phrase, not a bare nominative food noun, preserves “a certain amount.” The king’s quoted commands must not become a narrative that the sons already went and bought.''',
'44.2':'''Judah reports the chief’s oath. **Swōr** is past indicative; **mōsten** is first-plural past subjunctive in the reported prohibition, with plain **cuman**. It concerns permission to appear before the chief, not inability to travel. Supplied conditional **būtan** introduces the exception, and **bringen** keeps the brothers as first-plural subject. **Ūrne gingstan brōðor** is the accusative companion brought; the chief remains singular **hine**.''',
'44.3':'''The stipulated final verb in the condition is **wilt**, governing plain **sęndan**. The two main presents **gāð** and **bycgað** have subject **wē** and express the proposed future actions without an added “will.” **Ðā ðing** is accusative object of buying; invariant **ðe** has the complement role required by **beðurfon** inside the relative. The selected need frame is genitive, although ðe displays no case ending. Treating both main verbs as commands changes the speakers’ undertaking.''',
'44.4':'''The complete supplied phrase **earmlice wið mē dydon** means “you dealt badly with me.” Both **dydon** and **sægdon** agree with plural gē. **Hæfden** is second-plural past subjunctive in what they said; **mā brōðra** has a quantity word followed by genitive plural. It does not mean “more to the brothers,” nor does the report’s subjunctive establish that their statement was false.''',
'44.5':'''**Ūs ealle** retains “us all.” The two **hwæðer** clauses are indirect questions: whether the father **lifde**, and whether the speakers **hæfden** brothers. **Wiston** is plural past knowing; **wolde** presents the chief’s contemplated desire. Retain **giernan ðæs ðæt**: genitive ðæs is the thing desired, and the following content clause explains it. **Lǣdden** is first-plural past subjunctive, with their brother as accusative object. Omitting either question, all, or the genitive demonstrative loses a source control.''',
'44.6':'''Singular **sęnd** addresses Jacob. **Ðæt wē mægen bycgan** gives the purpose of sending the boy, and **ðæt wē ne forweorðen** gives the further purpose, avoiding starvation or death. The needed things retain the relative dependency explained in44.3. In the new condition the subject changes to **ic**: **bringe** is first-singular present subjunctive, and **sīe ic scyldig** is the resulting self-imprecation, “let me be guilty.” A simple indicative admission of guilt would lose that force.''',
'44.7':'''Use the entire supplied counterfactual frame. **Nǣre** is negative past subjunctive third singular with “this delay”; **wǣren** is first-plural past subjunctive auxiliary with past participle **cumen**. Thus the result is “we would have come a second time,” not merely “we were coming.” **Ōðre sīðe** is adverbial instrumental singular under Cook176; the noun hint sīð does not remove its ending. No article represents English “a.” An ordinary accusative phrase or a finite reading of cumen misses the stipulated construction.''',
'44.8':'''**Nīede** supplies necessity, while **sculon** governs dōn. The following **dōð, nimað, bringað** are plural commands. **Of ēowrum bętstum wæstmum** and **on fatum** use dative plurals; **ðǣm męnn** is the dative recipient without an added tō. **Lāc** is accusative plural “presents,” despite matching its singular spelling; honey is mass singular and **æcernu** is the selected accusative plural “acorns.” Do as you wish is not a statement that they have already acted.''',
'45.1':'''**Nimað** and **gāð** address the brothers collectively. The brother taken is singular accusative **ēowerne brōðor**, with the plural possessors expressed by ēowerne. **Tō ðǣm męnn** gives their destination. A singular command changes the audience; pluralizing brōðor changes the person they are to take.''',
'45.2':'''**Gedō** is a third-singular subjunctive wish, “may my Lord make him …,” not an imperative addressed to the Lord. It has accusative **hine** and agreeing predicate **glædne**, here gracious toward them. Purpose **āgiefe** restores the accusative brother to dative **ēow**; the relative says that the chief presently has him. **Eom berēafod** is passive, with genitive **mīnra bearna**, what Jacob is deprived of. Changing that genitive to a nominative subject would reverse the deprivation.''',
'45.3':'''The brothers took both the presents and double money, then travelled to Joseph. **Nōmon** and **fōron** are third-plural preterites. **Twīfeald feoh** is accusative mass singular, not a plural count of coins; retain the source’s ī and unmarked feoh. The whole supplied destination **tō Egypta lande tō Iosepe** distinguishes the land from Joseph as the person approached. It does not require an independently invented foreign-name declension.''',
'45.4':'''Joseph sees the men and speaks to one steward: **lǣd** and **gearca** are singular commands. In the purpose clause **hīe mægen etan**, the brothers become the third-plural subject; **mid mē** refers to Joseph. The supplied time phrase **tō middes dæges** means at noon, with genitive middes agreeing with dæges. The ability form is not a new finite “eat” form, and the noon phrase is not a command to use the ordinary dative after every tō.''',
'45.5':'''**Hē dyde** is the steward’s action. **Swā him boden wæs** is the supplied impersonal command passive: “as was commanded him.” **Him** remains dative addressee, not a nominative passive subject. Cook’s cited parallel has hiere beboden wæs; the model uses the separately attested command sense of bēodan. A personal passive paraphrase may be possible, but this exercise expressly supplies the impersonal target.''',
'45.6':'''The narrative’s plural **wurdon … āfǣred** describes the brothers becoming frightened; a predicative participle may remain uninflected. Inside their speech, **for ðǣm fēo** is causal, and **on ūrum saccum** is static location. **Wē sindon … lǣded** preserves the passive without adding an English-style have; base lǣded is licensed with plural wē. **Berēafie** takes accusative ūs and genitive possessions **ūrra assena / ūrra ðinga**. **Ūs selfe** is the object of **ðēowie**, here enslave, not serve. Reversing those roles reverses their fear.''',
'45.7':'''**Lēof** addresses the steward. **Biddað** is an assertion with wē, “we beseech,” although the spelling can also occur in a command. **Ðē** is the person beseeched; **ðū** is subject of subjunctive **hlyste**, which governs genitive **ūrre sprǣce**. **Bohton ūs hwǣte** means “we bought wheat for ourselves”: ūs is benefactive/reflexive dative, wheat the accusative object. The source gives no instruction to omit “for ourselves.” Confusing the two pronouns’ roles or making the speech accusative misses the assigned government.''',
'45.8':'''The first **cēapodon** and later **sealdon** are simple preterites with the anterior meanings “had traded” and “had given”; **ǣr** makes the earlier payment explicit. **Hāmweard wǣron** is the supplied homeward frame. **Undydon** says that the speakers opened their sacks; **fundon** then states their discovery. **Ðe** refers to the money previously given, not the sacks. Adding a perfect auxiliary is unnecessary, while removing the earlier-payment relation loses part of the meaning.''',
'45.9':'''**Brōhton** expresses the bringing back without requiring an auxiliary have. “Other silver” is a second object of that bringing; **mid tō cēapianne** is the supplied compressed instrument-and-purpose phrase, retaining the requested “with to trade” order. **Hwā** introduces the indirect question about the unknown person who **dyde** the money into the sacks. The movement/destination phrase **on ūre saccas** is accusative plural. Do not interpret the silver as a human trading partner or the final question as a new direct question.''',
'46.1':'''**Sibb sīe mid ēow** is a wish of peace. **Ondrǣdað ēow** is a negative plural command with reflexive dative, no expressed feared object. The two descriptions “your God” and “your father’s God” refer here to one deity, hence singular **sealde**. **Ēowres** agrees with genitive fæder; the following **ēow** is dative recipient of the treasure. The money is located **on ēowrum saccum**, dative plural. Making two gods or changing the possessive’s case changes the supplied interpretation.''',
'46.2':'''**Ðwōgon** is the contracted verb’s third-plural preterite: the brothers washed their accusative plural **fēt**. The subject then changes to singular **hē**, the steward, who **sealde** fodder to **hiera assum**, dative plural. **Hiera** is a possessor in both phrases; its unchanged spelling does not give the feet and asses the same grammatical role.''',
'46.3':'''**Lęgdon** is third-plural preterite of lęcgean, with accusative plural **lāc** and forð “forth.” The supplied **ongēan ðætte** clause means in readiness for Joseph’s entering. **Inēode** is past subjunctive third singular in that prospective frame; its identical indicative spelling elsewhere does not settle its mood here. A mere assertion that he had already entered would lose the preparation relation.''',
'46.4':'''Joseph is the singular recognizer and questioner; both accusative **hīe** forms refer to the brothers. **Oncnēow** is the preterite of recognition. In the indirect questions the subject is their father, with third-singular past subjunctives **wǣre** and **lifde**. The question asks whether he was well or alive; it does not assert that he was dead or make the brothers the subject of lifde.''',
'46.5':'''“Thy servant, our father” names one person, the singular subject of **is** and **leofað**. **Gesund** means well or healthy; **gīet** preserves “yet.” **Ðēow** is the selected strong servant noun, not the weak noun ðēowa or an adjective silently substituted for it. A present assertion of life is needed; past lifde would answer a different temporal question.''',
'46.6':'''Joseph first speaks of Benjamin in the identification question, then addresses him directly. Retain the requested neuter **ðis** in **is ðis se cnapa**. The relative **ðe** represents the boy mentioned earlier; **mē** is dative hearer after **foresægdon**, and no “of” is inserted. **Gemiltsie** is a third-singular subjunctive wish with dative **ðē**, “may God have compassion on you.” **Mīn sunu** is address, not the subject of that wish.''',
'46.7':'''Commanding **bēad** takes dative **his gerēfan**. Singular **gefyl** and **sęte** address that steward. **Mid hwǣte** supplies the sacks’ contents, while **ǣlces hiera feoh** means each one’s money: the money belongs to each brother. **On his āgenne sacc** is the accusative destination, each owner’s own sack. Making all the money belong to the steward or using a static dative for the placement changes the instructions.''',
'46.8':'''The two things to take and put are the silver cup and the wheat-price. Supplied **seolfrenne** agrees with masculine accusative singular **læfel**. **Ðæs hwǣtes** specifies the price, and the relative says that he gave it **ðē**, to thee: preserve the source’s singular addressee. The second imperative **dō** shares the coordinated objects without an inserted singular “it.” **Ðæs gingstan** is the substantival weak genitive, the youngest person as possessor of the sack.''',
'46.9':'''Retain all three circumstances: they travelled, were outside the city, and had gone part of the way. **Būtan byrig** is the supplied spatial outside phrase; **sumne dǣl ðæs weges** expresses distance travelled, and the simple preterite fōron can convey “had gone.” **Ārīs, gā, āsca** address one steward. Future temporal **ðonne ðū hīe fō** has second-singular present subjunctive, “when you have caught them.” In the indirect why-question, **wolden** is plural past subjunctive and **gōd mid yfele forgieldan** means repay good with evil. The evil is what is given in return.''',
'47.1':'''The cup is the singular subject of **wæs**. Relative **ðe** represents it as object of **forstǣlon**, past indicative second plural addressed to the brothers. **Mīnum hlāforde** is dative, the person to whom it was precious. The past plural’s **ǣ** differs from singular forstæl in47.8. The small isolated mark in the print does not supply an additional word or grammatical instruction.''',
'47.2':'''The steward did what was commanded him, using the same impersonal frame as45.5. **Fangen wǣron** is passive with the brothers as plural subject. Their following claim preserves the money, its discovery in the sacks, the return to the steward and the origin in their country. **Wēnst ðū ðæt … stǣlen** asks whether he thinks they stole the gold or silver. **Stǣlen** is first-plural past subjunctive; it supplies the questioned earlier theft without adding the omitted have. It is not an admission that they stole anything.''',
'47.3':'''**Sēc** is a singular command. **Sīe hē ofslægen** is a third-singular wish, “let him be slain”; it is not an instruction that the addressee kill himself. In **mid ðǣm ðe ðū hit finde**, mid governs the dative associate and invariant ðe resumes that person. **Finde** is second-singular present subjunctive in the optative/hypothetical relative under Cook195. The cup remains accusative hit.''',
'47.4':'''The free-choice phrase **mid swā hwǣm swā** identifies whichever person has the cup. **Hē** resumes that person as subject of singular **sīe**. The remaining brothers are plural **ðā ōðre** with **sīen**, and **clǣne** means clear of blame here. “Whoever I find it with” is an equivalent English rendering; making all the brothers guilty or making the indefinite person plural reverses the contrast.''',
'47.5':'''The search proceeds **fram ðǣm ieldestan oð ðone gingstan**: dative after fram, accusative after oð. **Funde** is the already taught and source-attested third-singular form of findan; it is not an error or necessarily second singular. **Fand** is also attested, but funde is the selected model. The cup is found in supplied **Beniamines sacce**, static dative; plural **gewęndon** then returns the brothers to the city. Do not make the steward the plural returning subject.''',
'47.6':'''**Iudas inēode fyrmest** says that Judah entered first, with his brothers. **Mid his brōðrum** describes his companions, not a plural subject of inēode. The next clause expressly has **hīe ealle** and plural **fēollon**: all fell together before Joseph. Both the leading entry and the collective prostration must be retained.''',
'47.7':'''The direct question keeps **gē** as the plural addressees and **woldon** as the final finite verb, governing plain **dōn**. **Swā** means “so/in that manner,” referring to the alleged conduct. A grammatical question with a different order can miss the explicit verb-last control; a singular modal would change the audience.''',
'47.8':'''The singular wish **sīe hē mīn ðēow** is restricted by the relative to the one who stole the cup. **Forstæl** is past indicative third singular, unlike plural forstǣlon in47.1. The rest receive plural command **gāð**, with supplied **frige**, free. Do not change the selected source spelling by silently adding vowel length, and do not extend the servitude to those being released.''',
'47.9':'''Judah addresses Joseph with singular **ðū āscodest**. **Ūs** is the plural people questioned; **hwæðer wē … hæfden** is the embedded question about their family, with first-plural past subjunctive. **Ǣr** retains the earlier time. Converting the embedded question into a present assertion, or making Jacob the person who asked, changes the report.''',
'47.10':'''The brothers are the subject of **habbað**, with accusative **ealdne fæder** and no indefinite article. The father then becomes singular subject of **hæfð** and **lufað**. **Ænne**, as supplied, agrees with accusative hine in the sense “him alone”; it is not the article omitted before father. **Ofer ūs ealle** preserves the comparison “above all of us.” Do not replace the singular youngest brother with several brothers.''',
'47.11':'''Keep the actual present **bīetst**, “thou commandest.” **Bringen** is first-plural present subjunctive in the commanded action; **gesēo** and **wite** are coordinated second-singular present subjunctives expressing its purpose. **Be ðǣm** means by that evidence, followed by the content **ðæt wē ne wǣren scēaweras**, with past subjunctive preserving “were.” The same-looking bīetst of bēatan is not meant. A silent backshift of commandest, or dropping the knowing clause, loses an explicit part of the prompt.''',
'48.1':'''Judah swore accusative **āðas** to dative **mīnum fæder**, without tō before that recipient. **Brōhte** is first-singular past subjunctive in the promised action. In the subsequent direct quotation **bringe** is first-singular present subjunctive after conditional būtan; **sīe ic ā scyldig** is the enduring self-imprecation. The father referred to as him in the narrative becomes **ðē** in the speech addressed to him. The change of person must not create a new recipient.''',
'48.2':'''Singular **lǣt** addresses Joseph. **Ðone cnapan** is accusative actor of plain infinitive **gān**: permit the boy to go home with his brothers. The second clause is an independent first-singular wish, **sīe ic ðīn ðēow**, with no repeated lexical “let.” **For hine** is substitution with accusative, “in his place.” Making Judah the person allowed to depart would reverse his offer.''',
'48.3':'''**Ne dearr … gān** expresses the speaker’s present refusal through lack of daring, with a plain infinitive. **Būtan ðǣm cnapan** means without the boy, a dative prepositional phrase; it is not the unless conjunction used elsewhere. Negative-purpose **ðȳ-lǣs-ðe … gesēo** means “lest I see,” with first-singular present subjunctive. The father is genitive possessor of accusative grief. The feared sight is prospective, not an event that has already occurred.''',
'48.4':'''Past **meahte** governs plain **diernan**; accusative **hine** refers reflexively to Joseph. **Lęng** modifies the duration of concealment. After **ādrāf … ūt**, the final clause gives the purpose that no foreign person be among them, with **wǣre** past subjunctive. **Nān … ne** is concordant negation. Losing either the expulsion or the purpose reduces the sentence to only part of the event.''',
'48.5':'''Joseph both **wēop** and **clēopode**. The latter retains the long ē of Cook’s exact clēopian hint; it does not rewrite the previously taught unmarked cleopian spelling. **Hlūdre stefne** is dative/instrumental manner, with a loud voice, without an extra preposition. The quoted **eom** identifies Joseph; **leofað … nū gīet?** asks whether the father still lives, without an English-style do auxiliary.''',
'48.6':'''**Ondrǣdað ēow nānes ðinges** combines reflexive dative ēow with genitive of the thing feared. **Ofðynce ēow** is third-singular impersonal subjunctive, “let it not grieve you”; the plural experiencers do not make its verb plural. **Sealdon** here means sold, not gave. **God sęnde** supplies the asserted agent without an added cleft “it was.” The supplied benefit phrase **for ēowerre ðearfe** is dative feminine singular, including the possessive ending. Replacing it with accusative ēowre would miss that agreement.''',
'48.7':'''Use the complete supplied frame. **Twā gēar** gives duration, while singular **hunger** governs **wæs**; **ofer ealle eorðan** is accusative extent without an article. In **gīet sculon fīf**, fīf stands for five further years and the source explicitly omits “there” and “be.” Do not invent an additional written copula. **On ðǣm** refers to those years; indefinite **mann** is singular subject of **mæg**. **Nāhwæðer ne ęrian ne rīpan** excludes both activities. “Neither … nor” must not become “either … or.”''',
'48.8':'''Plural **gāð, sęcgað, bēodað** address the brothers. In the supplied appointment clause, **mē** is the person appointed, **tō hlāforde** the role, and **eallum Egyptum** the people governed; the last phrase has neither an added article nor tō. The father is dative **him** after bēodað, then singular subject of subjunctives **cume** and **wunie**. The different addressees and actors must not be merged into a single plural command to dwell.''',
'48.9':'''**Dōð swā** is a plural command, “do thus.” The following **ðæt gē ne forweorðen** gives its negative purpose, so that they may not perish, with second-plural present subjunctive. The target is manner **swā**, not the object pronoun ðis “this.” An indicative statement that they are already perishing reverses the purpose.''',
'48.10':'''Both main verbs have plural **gē**: you see the stated circumstances and hear the speech. **Hū hit mid mē is** is the supplied state-of-affairs expression “how it is with me,” not mere physical accompaniment. **Hwæt ic tō ēow sprece** is what Joseph says to them: first-singular speaker, plural addressees. Equivalent English wording such as “how matters stand with me” is acceptable; replacing either embedded clause by a new direct question loses the sentence’s structure.'''
}
